From a49a5ae38e941b0c650f8f7f92b0496e1ffbba47 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 08:55:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban OOM follow-ups to #1942: no kernel-log read
 on a clean exit, own-scope note on rc 0

Review nits from PR #1942 (review 5887716688):

1. read_failure takes the dead worker's known exit code. On rc 0 it skips the
   per-pid journalctl -k lookup that ran inside the reclaim write txn for every
   dead worker with a known pid: a worker that exited cleanly was not the
   kernel's victim. _classify_dead_worker now classifies the exit first
   (_classify_dead_worker_exit, pure, already resolves the code from the
   authority, the reap registry, then the worker log's exit trailer) and passes
   rc 0 on a clean_exit. Its signature is compacted to offset FILE_LINES growth.
2. OomWatch's docstring says attribution is approximate: membership is judged
   against the start-time cgroup.procs snapshot and the kernel lookup returns
   only the window's last victim.
3. _own_scope_note no longer skips rc 0: a survivable command whose pipeline
   masks its own scope's OOM kill gets the same "a pipeline may hide its exit
   status" note as the run-scope path.
4. terminal_oom renders an unknown cap as "unknown" (as kanban_worker_scope
   does), not "MemoryMax unknown" inside "memory cap (...)"; `import time` moves
   to module scope.

Follow-up card t_01a1d3dce4d59355.
